 ## Background
-The method was introduced by [S1] to address the explosion of configuration cases in dimensions higher than 3D. In 3D, storing all 256 cases is feasible, but in 4D, the number of major cases grows rapidly (222 major cases vs. 256 configurations in 3D). The technique leverages the fact that major cases can be constructed from a union of edge-connected sub-cases, allowing for a more compact representation of the isosurface geometry in higher dimensions [S1].
